-- Rust binding for anthill.persistence (proposal 007 / 038).
--
-- THREE THINGS LIVE HERE, and they answer three different questions:
--   * `provides <Spec> language rust` + `operation_map` — WHICH HOST FUNCTION
--     realizes each storage operation. Asked once per OPERATION.
--   * `provides <Backend> language rust`               — WHICH BACKENDS this host
--     realizes at all. Asked once per CARRIER.
--   * `fact <Spec>[<Backend>]`                          — which store traits each
--     backend supplies.
--
-- ── the operations are COMMON, so they are declared and mapped ONCE ──────────
--
-- Every storage operation is one rust function over the store's STATE: it takes
-- the store as its first argument, resolves that VALUE to the mirror registered
-- for it (`Interpreter::register_mirror`) and drives the KB's persistence seam.
-- There is no per-backend variant to name — `FileStore` and `IndexedFileStore`
-- reach the same six — so mapping them per carrier would be six signatures copied
-- per backend to say the same thing twice.
--
-- This is NOT the spec-op registration WI-876 removed, and the difference is now
-- enforced rather than argued. That one was a MONOMORPHIC implementation (`i64`
-- comparison) on a polymorphic spec op, answering wrongly for carriers it could
-- not handle. These are genuinely polymorphic. But WI-876 had a SECOND finding
-- that polymorphism does not answer — a spec-level mapping used to make
-- `check_provider_operations` certify EVERY carrier of the spec, so an arbitrary
-- `entity ZzNotAStore(v: Int64)` could claim `fact NonMonotonicStore[ZzNotAStore]`
-- and load clean (MEASURED, on the first cut of this file). `op_backed` no longer
-- accepts a host mapping on the SPEC op as backing for a CARRIER (kb/typing.rs):
-- a mapping says the operation has an implementation, never that a given carrier
-- is realized. That is the question the blocks below answer.
--
-- ── which backends this host realizes, one line each ────────────────────────
--
-- `provides FileStore language rust` emits the `anthill.realization.Implementation`
-- fact that marks the carrier host-realized. `SqlStore` has no such block because
-- rustland implements no SQL backend in any form, so its provisions are REFUSED
-- rather than merely absent — and a backend added later must say so here to be
-- believed. That is also why the SQL store is no longer a standard-library sort
-- at all: it is a SHAPE, and it lives at `examples/sql-store/sql.anthill` (WI-934).
--
-- ── why the satisfaction facts live here and not in the stdlib (WI-931) ─────
--
-- Same rule as `geometry.anthill` one file over: a `fact Spec[Carrier]` may only
-- be declared in a closure where the spec's operations are BACKED (proposal 038).
-- `anthill.persistence` is language-agnostic and its operations are host
-- primitives, so in a stdlib-only load NOTHING backs `retract` — the claim was
-- unsound standing in the stdlib, and went unnoticed only because §6.3's
-- free-standing carriers were invisible to the check (WI-928).
namespace anthill.persistence
  provides Store language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs"
    operation_map {
      persist: "store_persist",
      flush: "store_flush",
      monotonicity: "store_monotonicity"
    }
  end

  provides NonMonotonicStore language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs"
    operation_map {
      retract: "store_retract",
      update: "store_update"
    }
  end

  provides QueryableStore language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/src/eval/builtins.rs"
    operation_map {
      retrieve: "store_retrieve"
    }
  end
end

namespace anthill.persistence.filesystem
  import anthill.persistence.{QueryableStore, NonMonotonicStore}

  provides FileStore language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/file_store.rs"
  end

  provides IndexedFileStore language rust
    artifact "rustland/anthill-core/src/persistence/indexed_file_store.rs"
  end

  -- The traits each backend supplies. NO `BulkStore` on either: its sole member
  -- `pull` has no anthill-callable implementation at all (WI-932).
  fact NonMonotonicStore[FileStore]           -- filesystem can retract (053 / 007 §2)

  fact QueryableStore[IndexedFileStore]
  fact NonMonotonicStore[IndexedFileStore]    -- span-based retract in place
end
